{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/66323-snipers-attacked-a-silicon-valley-power-station-last-year-and-nobody-told-us","id":66323,"title":"Snipers Attacked A Silicon Valley Power Station Last Year And Nobody Told Us","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Additional Discussion","Off-Topic"],"posts":[{"date":"2014-02-06T01:24:41-08:00","id":66323,"title":"Snipers Attacked A Silicon Valley Power Station Last Year And Nobody Told Us","content":"<a href=\"http://sfist.com/2014/02/05/pge_metcalf_station_terrorist_attac.php\" target=\"_blank\">http://sfist.com/2014/02/05/pge_metc...rist_attac.php</a> Around 1 a.m. on April 16th last year, a team of attackers cut phone likes and took out 17 power transformers at a PG&amp;E substation south of San Jose, nearly causing a blackout throughout Silicon Valley. According to the former chairman of the Federal Energy Regulatory Commission, it was the &quot;most significant incident of domestic terrorism&quot; involving the nation's power grid. No one has been charged or arrested in the attack.<br /> The Wall Street Journal, <a href=\"http://online.wsj.com/news/articles/SB10001424052702304851104579359141941621778?mg=reno64-wsj&amp;url=http%3A%2F%2Fonline.wsj.com%2Farticle%2FSB10001424052702304851104579359141941621778.html\" target=\"_blank\">broke news</a> of the attack today, pieced together a timeline of events that unfolded in the middle of the night, just yards from Highway 101:<div style=\"margin-left:40px\"><i>The attack began just before 1 a.m. on April 16 last year, when someone slipped into an underground vault not far from a busy freeway and cut telephone cables.</i> <i>Within half an hour, snipers opened fire on a nearby electrical substation. Shooting for 19 minutes, they surgically knocked out 17 giant transformers that funnel power to Silicon Valley. A minute before a police car arrived, the shooters disappeared into the night.</i><br /> </div>The team of gunmen knew what they were doing in the caper too: they targeted oil-filled cooling systems that bled out until the transformers overheated and crashed rather than shooting up the explosion-prone transformers themselves. The crash triggered an alarm at a PG&amp;E control center 90 miles away, but police on the scene around 1:51 a.m. couldn't get past a locked fence and assumed everything was fine. <br /> Electric-grid officials, meanwhile, scrambled to avoid a blackout by re-routing power from other plants in Silicon Valley. It still took utility workers nearly a month to bring the Metcalf Transmission station back online.<br /> Nearly a year later and FBI officials in San Francisco tell the WSJ, the Bureau doesn't think a terrorist organization was behind the attack, but they are &quot;continuing to sift through the evidence.&quot; <b>PG&amp;E's official statement claimed it was the work of vandals, but made no mention of the nearly 100 fingerprint-free shell casings that were found at the scene.</b> Former FERC chairmain Jon Wellinghoff, meanwhile, saw fit to go public with the details after military experts confirmed the scene looks like a professional job.<br /> At a recent security conference, one former PG&amp;E vice president of transmission put it bluntly: <b>&quot;This wasn't an incident where Billy-Bob and Joe decided, after a few brewskis, to come in and shoot up a substation,&quot; Mark Johnson said in a presentation. &quot;This was an event that was well thought out, well planned and they targeted certain components.&quot;</b> PG&amp;E won't discuss any further details, fearing potential copycat bands of Billy-Bobs and/or unnamed terrorist organizations.<br /> Transmission stations like Metcalf are crucial links in the power grid, which has utility officials scared that anyone could come along and cause a blackout like the one that <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Northeast_blackout_of_2003\" target=\"_blank\">crippled the North East in 2003</a>. Although Wellinghoff is sounding the alarm here, more level heads from the not-for-profit <a href=\"http://www.nerc.com/Pages/default.aspx\" target=\"_blank\">North American Electric Reliability Corp.</a> claimed in a very customer support-sounding response that even if several substations crashed, most people would get their power back in a few hours. Which will be helpful when you want to read about it online a year later.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/604-shock","name":"Shock","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar604_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1071"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-02-06T08:04:26-08:00","id":66487,"title":"","content":"Just a couple points... Calling them snipers is a bit inaccurate since snipers generally use few exact shots to produce the desired effect. While there *are* sniper rifles that use rounds that match the 7.62 casings found, it's not an absolute Second while they were smart enough to open the relay box and disable phone lines* they were not smart enough to avoid getting caught on camera. From that all they can identify about the weapons is that they were a longarm.<br /> <br /> Second the bigger issue that I have is that when the cop team was dispatched to a downed transformer site they decided that everything was fine and dandy because the gate was locked without any inspection or evaluation. There could have been a dead kid in there for all they knew.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> *-note that in a secure system this is a really bad idea, as it will actually trip the alarms rather than silence them.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Odd_Canuck is not a topical medication or food product and is not to be taken internally or seriously.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/31-odd_canuck","name":"Odd_Canuck","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar31_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1474"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-02-06T09:13:59-08:00","id":66530,"title":"","content":"Third, it's a blog post. Still, Foreign Policy had a story on, same as Wall Street Journal so I thought it was worth reporting. <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/604-shock","name":"Shock","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar604_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1071"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-02-12T16:09:25-08:00","id":70901,"title":"","content":"There's a lot of stuff we don't tell anyone (often, not because they're secret but because nobody really gives a shit when we report it and the media missed it when it happened). You'd be amazed how many times we almost went to full nuclear war in the Cold War and just never told anyone. Ever. (And most of those incidents aren't classified anymore, they just read very boring so you don't realize just quite what happened.)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2058-nobody","name":"Nobody","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"199"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-02-12T18:21:24-08:00","id":70952,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nobody</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/66323-snipers-attacked-a-silicon-valley-power-station-last-year-and-nobody-told-us?p=70901#post70901\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">There's a lot of stuff we don't tell anyone (often, not because they're secret but because nobody really gives a shit when we report it and the media missed it when it happened). You'd be amazed how many times we almost went to full nuclear war in the Cold War and just never told anyone. Ever. (And most of those incidents aren't classified anymore, they just read very boring so you don't realize just quite what happened.)</div> </div> </div> </div>It's kinda like that anytime you get into anything jargon filled or specific that can't readily be broken down a very simple but dramatic view/event that can capture the imagination. It also might explain why things are often comically wrong in reports that can be rendered dramatic with the right keywords. <a href=\"http://www.huffingtonpost.com/harvey-wasserman/us-sailors-sick-from-fuku_b_4759831.html\" target=\"_blank\">This </a>comes to mind.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Odd_Canuck is not a topical medication or food product and is not to be taken internally or seriously.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/31-odd_canuck","name":"Odd_Canuck","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar31_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1474"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-02-13T05:39:36-08:00","id":71196,"title":"","content":"I don't believe that many in the past couple of generations are really aware of just how incredibly bad dropping a nuke on a large city is. WWII is a thing of the past and viewed from too far a &quot;distance.&quot; And whenever the idea pops up today it's a thing of science fiction. And it's not just here in the US. I recall reading a poll that many Indians that using nukes is not only inevitable against Pakistan, but warranted. It's scary","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Mankind was once an endangered species. It will likely be so again. And mankind will only have itself to blame.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/144-shadowflame","name":"Shadowflame","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar144_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-02-13T11:19:05-08:00","id":71453,"title":"","content":"So looking forward to the day India and Pakistan need to be &quot;contained&quot;. That's sarcasm for those that missed it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/OnyxGraphics/OnyxMod.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /> <span style=\"font-family:georgia\"><span style=\"color:#B22222\"><b>Onyx Path Forum Administrator</b></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#FF0000\"><b><span style=\"font-family:comic sans ms\">Posts in this color are moderator posts</span></b></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:comic sans ms\"><span style=\"color:#00FF00\">Posts in this color mean a Great Old One has driven me mad.</span></span><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/onyx-path-announcements/206-forum-rules\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Forum Terms of Use</b></a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/contact-us\" target=\"_blank\">the Contact Us link</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11-darksider","name":"Darksider","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11_2.jpeg","title":"Forum Administrator","rank":6,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1692"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-02-13T11:50:42-08:00","id":71482,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Shadowflame</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/66323-snipers-attacked-a-silicon-valley-power-station-last-year-and-nobody-told-us?p=71196#post71196\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I don't believe that many in the past couple of generations are really aware of just how incredibly bad dropping a nuke on a large city is. WWII is a thing of the past and viewed from too far a &quot;distance.&quot; And whenever the idea pops up today it's a thing of science fiction. And it's not just here in the US. I recall reading a poll that many Indians that using nukes is not only inevitable against Pakistan, but warranted. It's scary</div> </div> </div> </div>See I can kinda-sorta understand and live with that. It's the ignorance of the scale and the duration that irks me. A power plant going poof, even as badly as fukushima or even chernobyl doesn't have the scale of damage or the long term impact. Unfortunately they're running the wrong way with it and assuming that because it has the word nuclear in it, it means the area is destroyed and completely uninhabitable.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Odd_Canuck is not a topical medication or food product and is not to be taken internally or seriously.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/31-odd_canuck","name":"Odd_Canuck","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar31_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1474"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-02-13T16:05:35-08:00","id":71686,"title":"","content":"I disagree with that as strongly as I can. The three worst radioactive areas; <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kyshtym_disaster\" target=\"_blank\">Kyshtym disaster</a>; the <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chernobyl_disaster\" target=\"_blank\">Chernobyl disaster</a> and the <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fukushima_Daiichi_nuclear_disaster\" target=\"_blank\">Fukushima disaster</a> are now areas that aren't livable. The Chernobyl Exclusion Zone reportedly isn't safe for another 20,000 years. The Khystym area is sometimes referred to as the &quot;Forbidden Zone&quot; and if you have to drive anywhere near, you're advised to keep the windows up, the vents closed and you don't stop until you're safely passed. (Or so I was told.)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Mankind was once an endangered species. It will likely be so again. And mankind will only have itself to blame.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/144-shadowflame","name":"Shadowflame","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar144_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-02-13T22:11:57-08:00","id":71820,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Shadowflame</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/66323-snipers-attacked-a-silicon-valley-power-station-last-year-and-nobody-told-us?p=71686#post71686\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I disagree with that as strongly as I can. The three worst radioactive areas; <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kyshtym_disaster\" target=\"_blank\">Kyshtym disaster</a>; the <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chernobyl_disaster\" target=\"_blank\">Chernobyl disaster</a> and the <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fukushima_Daiichi_nuclear_disaster\" target=\"_blank\">Fukushima disaster</a> are now areas that aren't livable. The Chernobyl Exclusion Zone reportedly isn't safe for another 20,000 years. The Khystym area is sometimes referred to as the &quot;Forbidden Zone&quot; and if you have to drive anywhere near, you're advised to keep the windows up, the vents closed and you don't stop until you're safely passed. (Or so I was told.)</div> </div> </div> </div>... you do know that people are moving back into the Chernobyl areas without issue? Outside of some hotspots (which should be marked better) and outside of the sarcophagus and the remains of the actual plant it's now within the US/Canada safe guidelines for radiation exposure. Granted, it's going to take that 20,000 for the Strontium-90 and the Caesium-137 to decay to average background levels, but in another 30 years (again outside of the hotspots) it's going to be not significantly worse than living in Denver.<br /> <br /> Fukushima spat out a very very tiny amount of Caesium and primarily iodine isotopes. Which is good and bad. iodine is the particularly nasty one for animals and humans because it sticks in the body(specifically the thyroid gland). On the other hand, it has a half life of just over 8 days. Meaning that while there was a lot of radiation at first, every 8 days the amount of radiation released by the iodine is halve<span style=\"font-family:Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif\"><span style=\"color:#333333\"><span style=\"font-size:11px\">d.</span></span></span>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Odd_Canuck is not a topical medication or food product and is not to be taken internally or seriously.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/31-odd_canuck","name":"Odd_Canuck","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar31_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1474"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-02-14T01:50:22-08:00","id":71872,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Odd_Canuck</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/66323-snipers-attacked-a-silicon-valley-power-station-last-year-and-nobody-told-us?p=71820#post71820\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">... you do know that people are moving back into the Chernobyl areas without issue? Outside of some hotspots (which should be marked better) and outside of the sarcophagus and the remains of the actual plant it's now within the US/Canada safe guidelines for radiation exposure. Granted, it's going to take that 20,000 for the Strontium-90 and the Caesium-137 to decay to average background levels, but in another 30 years (again outside of the hotspots) it's going to be not significantly worse than living in Denver.<br /> <br /> Fukushima spat out a very very tiny amount of Caesium and primarily iodine isotopes. Which is good and bad. iodine is the particularly nasty one for animals and humans because it sticks in the body(specifically the thyroid gland). On the other hand, it has a half life of just over 8 days. Meaning that while there was a lot of radiation at first, every 8 days the amount of radiation released by the iodine is halve<span style=\"font-family:Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif\"><span style=\"color:#333333\"><span style=\"font-size:11px\">d.</span></span></span></div> </div> </div> </div>I would be more worried about overfishing, acidification of the oceans, and the like over Fukushima. There is a very real possibility that there simply won't be any fish in the sea at the rate that we are killing them off.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/604-shock","name":"Shock","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar604_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1071"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-02-14T05:30:40-08:00","id":71918,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Shock</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/66323-snipers-attacked-a-silicon-valley-power-station-last-year-and-nobody-told-us?p=71872#post71872\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I would be more worried about overfishing, acidification of the oceans, and the like over Fukushima. There is a very real possibility that there simply won't be any fish in the sea at the rate that we are killing them off.</div> </div> </div> </div>Now granted I'm going with the Navy's studies... so they're slightly biased but... turns out the ocean is big. REALLY fucking big. Turns out that we are dumping a horrible amount of trash and waste into the ocean and creating some patches on the surface that are pretty terrible, but most the volume of the ocean just doesn't give a shit about us. Overfishing tends to be a localized problem; when you move on from that area, the fish come back (eventually), barring external forces like that bay in Maryland.<br /> <br /> The problem is scale... we just have not affected the ocean enough yet. I propose a strict regime of toxic waste dumping and dynamite fishing for the next two hundred years or so! Eventually, if we get many, many more people involved in this effort than we have now, we can probably defile the Pacific ocean completely and utterly. Then, when our children's children's children have finished that work, we can move on to the next oceans.<br /> <br /> Now, that part being somewhat tongue-in-cheek, we should still undertake efforts to clean up the messes we've made, but we have to keep in perspective what we've done and what can be done to fix it (sometimes just leaving it alone will fix it, if you can stop adding to the source of the problem). The ocean can clean itself up quite a bit.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2058-nobody","name":"Nobody","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"199"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-02-14T06:03:30-08:00","id":71927,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nobody</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/66323-snipers-attacked-a-silicon-valley-power-station-last-year-and-nobody-told-us?p=71918#post71918\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Now granted I'm going with the Navy's studies... so they're slightly biased but... turns out the ocean is big. REALLY fucking big. Turns out that we are dumping a horrible amount of trash and waste into the ocean and creating some patches on the surface that are pretty terrible, but most the volume of the ocean just doesn't give a shit about us. Overfishing tends to be a localized problem; when you move on from that area, the fish come back (eventually), barring external forces like that bay in Maryland.<br /> <br /> Now, that part being somewhat tongue-in-cheek, we should still undertake efforts to clean up the messes we've made, but we have to keep in perspective what we've done and what can be done to fix it (sometimes just leaving it alone will fix it, if you can stop adding to the source of the problem). The ocean can clean itself up quite a bit.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm not exactly contradicting you, I want to make that clear. You haven't said anything there that I am calling factually incorrect... but I think it requires some clarification, some context that provides meaning to the facts. <br /> <br /> The ocean is really big. Much of its volume is also really empty. Life exists in different concentrations throughout that volume, within microhabitats that are suitable for individual species. One of the big problems is that the noticeable and negative human impact tends to overlap the microhabitats that contain a great deal of that biodiversity pretty strongly. Patches of garbage on the surface are a very small volume of the total ocean, but they are large swathes of the depth that photosynthetic microbial life tends to inhabit. Coastal areas where there is a measurable change in water chemistry are a small mass relative to the entire ocean, but the biodiversity and mass or living organisms is higher there by an order of magnitude than it is at a middling depth in the middle of the map. <br /> <br /> Many of these species are integral to the survival of the species they interact with or encounter; changes within the population of one produce effects in the entire localized ecosystem. Given time, this tends to result in reduced population diversity and the breakdown of more complicated intraspecies relationships. Something that can sometimes be a result of natural events as well, but not generally as rapidly or as widespread as human involvement. <br /> <br /> Fish (or any species) bouncing back if left alone tends to be closely related to that concept of microhabitat. Some species are more adaptable or more prolific and can see population regeneration given time and a reduction of whatever catalyst prompted the decline to begin with. Others are too localized, too specialized or not adaptable enough to ride it out, and are significantly, sometimes irreparably, affected. Overfishing being a localized problem is a statement that is too small in its scope to really explain what is happening in most instances. The ramifications of the local problem become widespread, as the smaller areas change and, in turn, change the areas that they border upon. <br /> <br /> I tend to be a person who, due to my education and background, looks at these situations rationally. I am not given to sensationalism and I don't get my information on the topic from cable news networks. There are a number of very real, tangible problems that are affecting ocean systems around the world; the pacific garbage patch and rising tuna prices are not the ones that have me the most concerned. They're just the most visible and easiest for the general population with their long forgotten 8th grade biology lessons to get their head around.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/63-loxosceles","name":"Loxosceles","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-02-14T06:23:42-08:00","id":71939,"title":"","content":"<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Odd_Canuck</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/66323-snipers-attacked-a-silicon-valley-power-station-last-year-and-nobody-told-us?p=71820#post71820\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">... you do know that people are moving back into the Chernobyl areas without issue?</div> </div> </div> </div>Says who? Russia? I think there's a reason why they call it an &quot;exclusion&quot; zone. Granted, after hearing what's going on in Sochi about the Olympics) exposure to such things as contaminated water and soil might be considered the norm. Considering the cancer rates in those areas I certainly wouldn't risk raising a family let alone live in such areas myself.<br /> <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Effects_of_the_Chernobyl_disaster\" target=\"_blank\">Effects of the Chernobyl Disaster</a><br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Odd_Canuck</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/66323-snipers-attacked-a-silicon-valley-power-station-last-year-and-nobody-told-us?p=71820#post71820\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> Outside of some hotspots (which should be marked better) and outside of the sarcophagus and the remains of the actual plant it's now within the US/Canada safe guidelines for radiation exposure. Granted, it's going to take that 20,000 for the Strontium-90 and the Caesium-137 to decay to average background levels, but in another 30 years (again outside of the hotspots) it's going to be not significantly worse than living in Denver.</div> </div> </div> </div> That's less an endorsement and more a condemnation.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Odd_Canuck</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/66323-snipers-attacked-a-silicon-valley-power-station-last-year-and-nobody-told-us?p=71820#post71820\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Fukushima spat out a very very tiny amount of Caesium and primarily iodine isotopes. Which is good and bad. iodine is the particularly nasty one for animals and humans because it sticks in the body(specifically the thyroid gland). On the other hand, it has a half life of just over 8 days. Meaning that while there was a lot of radiation at first, every 8 days the amount of radiation released by the iodine is halve</div> </div> </div> </div>.<br /> And again this doesn't endorse nuclear power.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://www.cbc.ca/news/world/fukushima-residents-question-radiation-cleanup-effort-1.1856332\" target=\"_blank\">Fukushima Residents</a><br /> <br /> <i>\u201cWhatever happens, we will never have the kind of life we had before. It\u2019s clear that my grandchildren will never come here again.\u201d</i>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Mankind was once an endangered species. It will likely be so again. And mankind will only have itself to blame.</i>","edited":{"userid":144,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/144-shadowflame","user":"Shadowflame","date":". 02-14-2014, 09:26 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/144-shadowflame","name":"Shadowflame","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar144_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-02-14T06:44:13-08:00","id":71950,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Shadowflame</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/66323-snipers-attacked-a-silicon-valley-power-station-last-year-and-nobody-told-us?p=71939#post71939\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><i>\u201cWhatever happens, we will never have the kind of life we had before. It\u2019s clear that my grandchildren will never come here again.\u201d</i></div> </div> </div> </div>Well shit, if a single 71 year old man with no applicable credentials says he's not bringing his grandkids by, that clearly trumps physics. Suck it, physicals laws governi